de; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [2 pt] Yes: low fare. Whatever Airline Y chooses, Airline X earns more with low fare (for example 61 vs 49 if Airline Y chooses high fare, and 37 vs 32 if Airline Y chooses low fare).\n   - 1 pt: Identifies the dominant strategy\n   - 1 pt: Justifies by showing that the strategy pays more against each of the rival's choices\n(B) [1 pt] (Airline X: low fare, Airline Y: low fare).\n   - 1 pt: States the equilibrium strategy pair for both firms\n(C) [1 pt] $37 million for Airline X and $43 million for Airline Y.\n   - 1 pt: Reads both equilibrium payoffs from the matrix (answer)\n(D) [1 pt] At least one firm earns more by switching to its best response, so the outcome is not stable without enforcement.\n   - 1 pt: Explains that at least one firm has an incentive to deviate from that outcome (a better response exists), so it is not stable"},"set":{"explanation":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [2 pt] Yes: low fare. Whatever Airline Y chooses, Airline X earns more with low fare (for example 61 vs 49 if Airline Y chooses high fare, and 37 vs 32 if Airline Y chooses low fare).\n   - 1 pt: Identifies the dominant strategy\n   - 1 pt: Justifies by showing that the strategy pays more against each of the rival's choices\n(B) [1 pt] (Airline X: low fare, Airline Y: low fare).\n   - 1 pt: States the equilibrium strategy pair for both firms\n(C) [1 pt] \\$37 million for Airline X and \\$43 million for Airline Y.\n   - 1 pt: Reads both equilibrium payoffs from the matrix (answer)\n(D) [1 pt] At least one firm earns more by switching to its best response, so the outcome is not stable without enforcement.\n   - 1 pt: Explains that at least one firm has an incentive to deviate from that outcome (a better response exists), so it is not stable","explanation_en":"Reference answer and scoring notes (not official College Board scoring). Compare your response with this guide; no AP score is estimated.\n\n(A) [2 pt] Yes: low fare. Whatever Airline Y chooses, Airline X earns more with low fare (for example 61 vs 49 if Airline Y chooses high fare, and 37 vs 32 if Airline Y chooses low fare).\n   - 1 pt: Identifies the dominant strategy\n   - 1 pt: Justifies by showing that the strategy pays more against each of the rival's choices\n(B) [1 pt] (Airline X: low fare, Airline Y: low fare).\n   - 1 pt: States the equilibrium strategy pair for both firms\n(C) [1 pt] \\$37 million for Airline X and \\$43 million for Airline Y.\n   - 1 pt: Reads both equilibrium payoffs from the matrix (answer)\n(D) [1 pt] At least one firm earns more by switching to its best response, so the outcome is not stable without enforcement.\n   - 1 pt: Explains that at least one firm has an incentive to deviate from that outcome (a better response exists), so it is not stable"},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T08:09:42.908Z","issues":[],"alt":"Payoff matrix: Airline X (rows) and Airline Y (columns). Table with columns Airline X \\ Airline Y, high fare, low fare and 2 rows."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R4-07516ae5","fromVersionId":"4b6d1df5-30e9-4ebb-8ad8-84c5468a1f9f","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"micro-p2-f:ap_microeconomics-f05-k1","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"R4-2c02a8a7","problem_id":"2c02a8a7-220c-4e67-869b-97a63ee81f72","from_version_id":"cc62685a-827f-4372-acf5-ddef3f75a532","expect":{"explanation_en":"The total cost is the $12 daily rate times the number of rental days $d$, plus the $85 fixed fee. To stay within the budget, $85 + 12d \\le 205$ must hold. Subtracting 85 from both sides gives $12d \\le 120$, and dividing both sides by 12 gives $d \\le 10$. So the maximum rental is 10 days. Wrong answer B (9) comes from a division error in $12d \\le 120$, confusing it with 118 divided by 12. Wrong answer A (8) comes from a calculation slip in subtracting 85 from 205, using 96 instead of 120. Wrong answer D (12) comes from leaving out the $85 fixed fee when setting up the inequality and dividing 205 by 12 directly, giving a value close to 12."},"set":{"explanation_en":"The total cost is the \\$12 daily rate times the number of rental days $d$, plus the \\$85 fixed fee. To stay within the budget, $85 + 12d \\le 205$ must hold. Subtracting 85 from both sides gives $12d \\le 120$, and dividing both sides by 12 gives $d \\le 10$. So the maximum rental is 10 days. Wrong answer B (9) comes from a division error in $12d \\le 120$, confusing it with 118 divided by 12. Wrong answer A (8) comes from a calculation slip in subtracting 85 from 205, using 96 instead of 120. Wrong answer D (12) comes from leaving out the \\$85 fixed fee when setting up the inequality and dividing 205 by 12 directly, giving a value close to 12."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T08:09:42.908Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R4-2c02a8a7","fromVersionId":"cc62685a-827f-4372-acf5-ddef3f75a532","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R4-36ba74b4","problem_id":"36ba74b4-b671-4438-8f7c-a3144d72ecbf","from_version_id":"c4ff11ec-fc95-46fe-8f51-079423e76a78","expect":{"explanation_en":"Let s be the number of small paintings. Since there are 20 paintings in total, the number of large paintings is 20 - s. Restoring one small painting costs $240 and restoring one large painting costs $560, so the total cost of the small paintings is 240s and the total cost of the large paintings is 560(20 - s). Because the entire budget was used, the sum of these two costs must equal 8400. So the correct equation is 240s + 560(20 - s) = 8400, and the answer is A. B swaps the costs of the small and large paintings, C uses s instead of 20 - s for the number of large paintings, and D puts the total number of paintings on the right side instead of the total budget."},"set":{"explanation_en":"Let s be the number of small paintings. Since there are 20 paintings in total, the number of large paintings is 20 - s. Restoring one small painting costs \\$240 and restoring one large painting costs \\$560, so the total cost of the small paintings is 240s and the total cost of the large paintings is 560(20 - s). Because the entire budget was used, the sum of these two costs must equal 8400. So the correct equation is 240s + 560(20 - s) = 8400, and the answer is A. B swaps the costs of the small and large paintings, C uses s instead of 20 - s for the number of large paintings, and D puts the total number of paintings on the right side instead of the total budget."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T08:09:42.908Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R4-36ba74b4","fromVersionId":"c4ff11ec-fc95-46fe-8f51-079423e76a78","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R4-3a04d041","problem_id":"3a04d041-1457-4c4f-a9b3-15c240d9be98","from_version_id":"b61ae7c4-f0b4-45a2-8cc8-1c3a78f0955a","expect":{"explanation_en":"The sample mean is $205,000 and the margin of error is $8,000, so the confidence interval for the population mean runs from 205,000 - 8,000 = $197,000 to 205,000 + 8,000 = $213,000. The historian's claimed value of $210,000 lies within this interval (197,000 to 213,000), so the claim is plausible. Therefore, the answer is D."},"set":{"explanation_en":"The sample mean is \\$205,000 and the margin of error is \\$8,000, so the confidence interval for the population mean runs from 205,000 - 8,000 = \\$197,000 to 205,000 + 8,000 = \\$213,000. The historian's claimed value of \\$210,000 lies within this interval (197,000 to 213,000), so the claim is plausible. Therefore, the answer is D."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T08:09:42.926Z","issues":[],"alt":"Impressionist Landscape Painting Auction Prices — 자료: Sample size 50, Sample mean 205,000 dollars, Margin of error 8,000 dollars. Random sample of auction records from the last decade."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R4-3a04d041","fromVersionId":"b61ae7c4-f0b4-45a2-8cc8-1c3a78f0955a","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R4-4167fd94","problem_id":"4167fd94-3a67-4adc-8dfc-f6e80c87526d","from_version_id":"10367865-71ab-4131-8605-6aab1ed11c21","expect":{"passage":"A small bakery tracked its total weekly revenue and the portion of that revenue that came from bread sales, as shown in the table below.\n\nWeek | Total Revenue | Bread Sales Revenue\n1 | $2,400 | $600\n2 | $2,500 | $750\n3 | $2,800 | $840\n4 | $3,000 | $900","explanation":"Week 3의 빵 판매 수익이 총수익에서 차지하는 비율을 구하려면 Week 3 행의 두 값, 즉 총수익 $2,800과 빵 판매 수익 $840을 사용해야 한다. 계산하면 840/2800 × 100 = 30%이므로 정답은 30%(B)이다.\n\n각 오답의 발생 원인은 다음과 같다.\n- 25%(A): Week 1의 값(600/2400 = 25%)을 잘못 가져와 계산한 경우이다.\n- 35%(C): Week 3의 수치를 사용하지 않고 근처 값들을 어림잡아 반올림하거나, 840을 2400으로 나누는 등 다른 주의 총수익과 혼동하여 계산한 경우 나올 수 있는 값이다.\n- 40%(D): Week 4의 값(900/3000이 아니라 840/2100처럼 잘못된 분모를 사용)이나 자릿수를 잘못 적용해 나온 값이다.\n\n따라서 문제에서 요구하는 '주'(Week 3)와 '항목'(빵 판매 수익, 총수익)을 정확히 짚어 840 ÷ 2800 × 100 = 30%를 계산해야 정답을 얻을 수 있다.","explanation_en":"To find the share of total revenue that bread sales made up in Week 3, use the two values in the Week 3 row: total revenue of $2,800 and bread sales revenue of $840. Computing 840/2800 × 100 = 30%, so the answer is 30% (B).\n\nEach wrong answer arises as follows.\n- 25% (A): taking the Week 1 values (600/2400 = 25%) by mistake.\n- 35% (C): this can result from rounding nearby values instead of using the Week 3 figures, or from dividing 840 by 2400 and thus mixing in another week's total revenue.\n- 40% (D): this comes from using a wrong denominator, such as a Week 4 value (not 900/3000 but something like 840/2100), or from a place-value error.\n\nTherefore, you must pick out exactly the week (Week 3) and the items (bread sales revenue and total revenue) the question asks for and compute 840 ÷ 2800 × 100 = 30% to get the correct answer."},"set":{"passage":"A small bakery tracked its total weekly revenue and the portion of that revenue that came from bread sales, as shown in the table below.\n\nWeek | Total Revenue | Bread Sales Revenue\n1 | \\$2,400 | \\$600\n2 | \\$2,500 | \\$750\n3 | \\$2,800 | \\$840\n4 | \\$3,000 | \\$900","explanation":"Week 3의 빵 판매 수익이 총수익에서 차지하는 비율을 구하려면 Week 3 행의 두 값, 즉 총수익 \\$2,800과 빵 판매 수익 \\$840을 사용해야 한다. 계산하면 840/2800 × 100 = 30%이므로 정답은 30%(B)이다.\n\n각 오답의 발생 원인은 다음과 같다.\n- 25%(A): Week 1의 값(600/2400 = 25%)을 잘못 가져와 계산한 경우이다.\n- 35%(C): Week 3의 수치를 사용하지 않고 근처 값들을 어림잡아 반올림하거나, 840을 2400으로 나누는 등 다른 주의 총수익과 혼동하여 계산한 경우 나올 수 있는 값이다.\n- 40%(D): Week 4의 값(900/3000이 아니라 840/2100처럼 잘못된 분모를 사용)이나 자릿수를 잘못 적용해 나온 값이다.\n\n따라서 문제에서 요구하는 '주'(Week 3)와 '항목'(빵 판매 수익, 총수익)을 정확히 짚어 840 ÷ 2800 × 100 = 30%를 계산해야 정답을 얻을 수 있다.","explanation_en":"To find the share of total revenue that bread sales made up in Week 3, use the two values in the Week 3 row: total revenue of \\$2,800 and bread sales revenue of \\$840. Computing 840/2800 × 100 = 30%, so the answer is 30% (B).\n\nEach wrong answer arises as follows.\n- 25% (A): taking the Week 1 values (600/2400 = 25%) by mistake.\n- 35% (C): this can result from rounding nearby values instead of using the Week 3 figures, or from dividing 840 by 2400 and thus mixing in another week's total revenue.\n- 40% (D): this comes from using a wrong denominator, such as a Week 4 value (not 900/3000 but something like 840/2100), or from a place-value error.\n\nTherefore, you must pick out exactly the week (Week 3) and the items (bread sales revenue and total revenue) the question asks for and compute 840 ÷ 2800 × 100 = 30% to get the correct answer."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T08:09:42.928Z","issues":[],"alt":"Weekly Bakery Revenue — 표(3열 4행). 열: Week, Total Revenue (dollars), Bread Sales Revenue (dollars). 1 / 2,400 / 600; 2 / 2,500 / 750; 3 / 2,800 / 840; 4 / 3,000 / 900."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R4-4167fd94","fromVersionId":"10367865-71ab-4131-8605-6aab1ed11c21","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R4-4cc47e7c","problem_id":"4cc47e7c-e766-47d8-aa9b-ae16aeb96d71","from_version_id":"08bb7b4a-a8a3-4d54-883a-bfb6b3ccab4e","expect":{"passage":"A landscaping company charges a flat fee of $45 for a site visit, plus $25 for each hour of work. The total cost, in dollars, for a job that takes h hours of work can be represented by the equation C = 25h + 45.","explanation":"Substitute h = 3 into C = 25h + 45: C = 25(3) + 45 = 75 + 45 = 120. So the total cost is $120. Choice A (70) incorrectly computes 25(3) - 5 or similar miscalculation. Choice B (75) only accounts for the hourly charge (25 × 3) and omits the flat fee of $45. Choice D (150) incorrectly adds the flat fee twice or uses 25(3+3).","explanation_en":"Substitute h = 3 into C = 25h + 45: C = 25(3) + 45 = 75 + 45 = 120. So the total cost is $120, which makes C the correct choice. Choice A (70) comes from a miscalculation such as 25(3) - 5. Choice B (75) only counts the hourly charge (25 × 3) and leaves out the flat fee of $45. Choice D (150) comes from adding the flat fee twice or from computing 25(3+3)."},"set":{"passage":"A landscaping company charges a flat fee of \\$45 for a site visit, plus \\$25 for each hour of work. The total cost, in dollars, for a job that takes h hours of work can be represented by the equation C = 25h + 45.","explanation":"Substitute h = 3 into C = 25h + 45: C = 25(3) + 45 = 75 + 45 = 120. So the total cost is \\$120. Choice A (70) incorrectly computes 25(3) - 5 or similar miscalculation. Choice B (75) only accounts for the hourly charge (25 × 3) and omits the flat fee of \\$45. Choice D (150) incorrectly adds the flat fee twice or uses 25(3+3).","explanation_en":"Substitute h = 3 into C = 25h + 45: C = 25(3) + 45 = 75 + 45 = 120. So the total cost is \\$120, which makes C the correct choice. Choice A (70) comes from a miscalculation such as 25(3) - 5. Choice B (75) only counts the hourly charge (25 × 3) and leaves out the flat fee of \\$45. Choice D (150) comes from adding the flat fee twice or from computing 25(3+3)."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T08:09:42.938Z","issues":[],"alt":"좌표평면(x 0~8, y 0~220), 가로축 Time (hours), 세로축 Cost (dollars). C = 25h + 45: y = 25x + 45."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R4-4cc47e7c","fromVersionId":"08bb7b4a-a8a3-4d54-883a-bfb6b3ccab4e","fromVersionNo":2}},{"patch_id":"R4-4e2a7d0c","problem_id":"4e2a7d0c-54f9-412f-85ed-12d1e2339e91","from_version_id":"80895a0e-bb00-4053-be17-9f759a718c63","expect":{"passage":"An art historian studying 19th-century landscape paintings wants to estimate the average auction price of paintings by a particular regional school of painters. She obtained a random sample of 60 auction records from that school and found a sample mean price of $84,000, with a margin of error of $6,500 at the 95% confidence level."},"set":{"passage":"An art historian studying 19th-century landscape paintings wants to estimate the average auction price of paintings by a particular regional school of painters. She obtained a random sample of 60 auction records from that school and found a sample mean price of \\$84,000, with a margin of error of \\$6,500 at the 95% confidence level."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T08:09:42.939Z","issues":[],"alt":"Auction Price Survey of Regional Landscape Paintings — 자료: Sample size 60, Sample mean auction price 84,000 dollars, Margin of error (95% confidence) 6,500 dollars. random sample of auction records from the regional school of painters."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R4-4e2a7d0c","fromVersionId":"80895a0e-bb00-4053-be17-9f759a718c63","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R4-8c87572d","problem_id":"8c87572d-4516-40ce-bedd-d51e92d01ea4","from_version_id":"1ae63367-07ca-4c4c-8f35-9aa009b04ecd","expect":{"explanation_en":"The graph is a line through the origin P(0, 0) and the point Q(50, 500). The $x$-intercept is the point where the graph meets the $x$-axis, that is, the value of $x$ when $y=0$. On this graph that point is the origin (0, 0), so the $x$-intercept is $0$. In context, $x$ is the number of tickets sold and $y$ is the total revenue in dollars, so an $x$-intercept of 0 means that selling 0 tickets (that is, no sales) brings in $0 in revenue. Therefore the answer is D. In A, 50 is just one value of ticket sales shown on the graph (the $x$-coordinate of point Q), not the $x$-intercept, and a fixed-cost idea does not fit this graph, which passes through the origin with an intercept of 0. In B, 500 is not the $y$-intercept but the $y$-coordinate of point Q (total revenue), and it is not the $x$-intercept either. In C, 10 is the slope of the graph (the price per ticket), not the $x$-intercept."},"set":{"explanation_en":"The graph is a line through the origin P(0, 0) and the point Q(50, 500). The $x$-intercept is the point where the graph meets the $x$-axis, that is, the value of $x$ when $y=0$. On this graph that point is the origin (0, 0), so the $x$-intercept is $0$. In context, $x$ is the number of tickets sold and $y$ is the total revenue in dollars, so an $x$-intercept of 0 means that selling 0 tickets (that is, no sales) brings in \\$0 in revenue. Therefore the answer is D. In A, 50 is just one value of ticket sales shown on the graph (the $x$-coordinate of point Q), not the $x$-intercept, and a fixed-cost idea does not fit this graph, which passes through the origin with an intercept of 0. In B, 500 is not the $y$-intercept but the $y$-coordinate of point Q (total revenue), and it is not the $x$-intercept either. In C, 10 is the slope of the graph (the price per ticket), not the $x$-intercept."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T08:09:42.945Z","issues":[],"alt":"좌표평면(x -5~60, y -50~550), 가로축 Tickets sold, 세로축 Revenue (dollars). g: y = 10x; 점 P (0, 0); 점 Q (50, 500)."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R4-8c87572d","fromVersionId":"1ae63367-07ca-4c4c-8f35-9aa009b04ecd","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R4-acb13fe4","problem_id":"acb13fe4-4107-43fd-b157-f8b50c884ee4","from_version_id":"7ae78ef2-a2b8-46a3-9b72-55acc8b73924","expect":{"passage":"A tailor is adjusting the price structure for custom suit alterations. The tailor charges a base fee for taking initial measurements plus an hourly rate for the actual alteration work. For a job that took 3 hours, the total charge was $145. For a job that took 5 hours, the total charge was $205."},"set":{"passage":"A tailor is adjusting the price structure for custom suit alterations. The tailor charges a base fee for taking initial measurements plus an hourly rate for the actual alteration work. For a job that took 3 hours, the total charge was \\$145. For a job that took 5 hours, the total charge was \\$205."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T08:09:42.946Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R4-acb13fe4","fromVersionId":"7ae78ef2-a2b8-46a3-9b72-55acc8b73924","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R4-cc21ca06","problem_id":"cc21ca06-e460-4b7e-982f-79c67fbc1d26","from_version_id":"edb82bd8-b8c3-4f84-b2b9-05a6f2f9dec9","expect":{"passage":"A city's public transit authority is analyzing fare revenue from bus and train riders. On a given day, each bus rider pays $2.50 and each train rider pays $3.50. The authority wants to know the combinations of bus riders $x$ (in thousands) and train riders $y$ (in thousands) that produce daily fare revenue of at least 70 (thousand dollars) while keeping total ridership at most 25 (thousand riders). In the figure, line $r$ represents $2.5x + 3.5y = 70$ and line $t$ represents $x + y = 25$, where $x$ and $y$ are measured in thousands of riders. The solution set consists of all points $(x, y)$ that lie on or above line $r$ and on or below line $t$."},"set":{"passage":"A city's public transit authority is analyzing fare revenue from bus and train riders. On a given day, each bus rider pays \\$2.50 and each train rider pays \\$3.50. The authority wants to know the combinations of bus riders $x$ (in thousands) and train riders $y$ (in thousands) that produce daily fare revenue of at least 70 (thousand dollars) while keeping total ridership at most 25 (thousand riders). In the figure, line $r$ represents $2.5x + 3.5y = 70$ and line $t$ represents $x + y = 25$, where $x$ and $y$ are measured in thousands of riders. The solution set consists of all points $(x, y)$ that lie on or above line $r$ and on or below line $t$."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T08:09:42.950Z","issues":[],"alt":"좌표평면(x 0~30, y 0~30), 가로축 Bus riders x (thousands), 세로축 Train riders y (thousands). 직선 r: y = −0.71x + 20; 직선 t: y = −x + 25."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R4-cc21ca06","fromVersionId":"edb82bd8-b8c3-4f84-b2b9-05a6f2f9dec9","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R4-e524ccce","problem_id":"e524ccce-42c7-4367-865c-2c89d0ffc235","from_version_id":"f57429c5-a4cc-4f86-a7bc-ea4e24f4068c","expect":{"explanation_en":"The year 1898 is 8 years after 1890, so $x = 8$. Substituting this into the regression equation $y = 1.8x + 24$ gives $y = 1.8(8) + 24 = 14.4 + 24 = 38.4$. So the predicted average monthly wage is $38.40, and the answer is B. Choice A (32.40) is a value from calculating 9 years instead of $x=8$ or from miscalculating the slope product, choice C (14.40) is what you get by giving only the result of multiplying by the slope without adding the constant 24, and choice D (43.20) is a value from wrongly calculating x as 10 years (the year 1900)."},"set":{"explanation_en":"The year 1898 is 8 years after 1890, so $x = 8$. Substituting this into the regression equation $y = 1.8x + 24$ gives $y = 1.8(8) + 24 = 14.4 + 24 = 38.4$. So the predicted average monthly wage is \\$38.40, and the answer is B. Choice A (32.40) is a value from calculating 9 years instead of $x=8$ or from miscalculating the slope product, choice C (14.40) is what you get by giving only the result of multiplying by the slope without adding the constant 24, and choice D (43.20) is a value from wrongly calculating x as 10 years (the year 1900)."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T08:09:42.951Z","issues":[],"alt":"산점도(점 11개), 가로축 Years since 1890 (x), 세로축 Average monthly wage (dollars), 추세선 y = 1.8x + 24."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R4-e524ccce","fromVersionId":"f57429c5-a4cc-4f86-a7bc-ea4e24f4068c","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R4-f050446a","problem_id":"f050446a-06e6-476b-89a1-66e2aa2180e0","from_version_id":"8232f998-be70-4067-8cb2-34d28a526317","expect":{"explanation_en":"January revenue is $2500 and March revenue is $3000. The percent increase is (3000-2500)/2500 × 100 = 500/2500 × 100 = 20%. Therefore, the answer is B. A (10%) uses the difference between February and January (250/2500=10%) rather than the correct difference (and it is not 500/3000≈16.7% either). C (25%) is a calculation error that uses the wrong base value, such as dividing 500 by 2000. D (44%) combines two other values instead of using January and March, an error of calculating from a month other than March."},"set":{"explanation_en":"January revenue is \\$2500 and March revenue is \\$3000. The percent increase is (3000-2500)/2500 × 100 = 500/2500 × 100 = 20%. Therefore, the answer is B. A (10%) uses the difference between February and January (250/2500=10%) rather than the correct difference (and it is not 500/3000≈16.7% either). C (25%) is a calculation error that uses the wrong base value, such as dividing 500 by 2000. D (44%) combines two other values instead of using January and March, an error of calculating from a month other than March."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T08:09:42.952Z","issues":[],"alt":"막대그래프, 세로축 Revenue (dollars). Revenue: January 2,500, February 2,750, March 3,000, April 2,900."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R4-f050446a","fromVersionId":"8232f998-be70-4067-8cb2-34d28a526317","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R4-f9c182ee","problem_id":"f9c182ee-47d9-49da-8cec-192e7ee610ee","from_version_id":"2a870125-0c95-424a-96a8-bb300372443e","expect":{"passage":"A community recycling center pays residents for aluminum cans and glass bottles. The center pays $0.05 per can and $0.10 per bottle. A resident wants to earn more than $15 by bringing in a combination of $x$ cans and $y$ bottles. The equation $0.05x + 0.10y = 15$ represents the combinations of $x$ and $y$ for which the resident earns exactly $15.","explanation":"한 캔당 $0.05, 한 병당 $0.10 을 받으므로 총 수입은 $0.05x + 0.10y$ 이다. \"15달러보다 많이\" 벌어야 하므로 등호 없는 엄격한 부등식이며, 그래프의 경계선 위쪽(더 큰 값)을 향해야 하므로 $0.05x + 0.10y > 15$ 이다. 따라서 정답은 C번이다.","explanation_en":"The resident receives $0.05 per can and $0.10 per bottle, so total earnings are $0.05x + 0.10y$. The resident must earn \"more than 15 dollars,\" so the inequality is strict with no equality, and it must point toward the region above the boundary line on the graph (larger values), giving $0.05x + 0.10y > 15$. Therefore, the answer is C."},"set":{"passage":"A community recycling center pays residents for aluminum cans and glass bottles. The center pays \\$0.05 per can and \\$0.10 per bottle. A resident wants to earn more than \\$15 by bringing in a combination of $x$ cans and $y$ bottles. The equation $0.05x + 0.10y = 15$ represents the combinations of $x$ and $y$ for which the resident earns exactly \\$15.","explanation":"한 캔당 \\$0.05, 한 병당 \\$0.10 을 받으므로 총 수입은 $0.05x + 0.10y$ 이다. \"15달러보다 많이\" 벌어야 하므로 등호 없는 엄격한 부등식이며, 그래프의 경계선 위쪽(더 큰 값)을 향해야 하므로 $0.05x + 0.10y > 15$ 이다. 따라서 정답은 C번이다.","explanation_en":"The resident receives \\$0.05 per can and \\$0.10 per bottle, so total earnings are $0.05x + 0.10y$. The resident must earn \"more than 15 dollars,\" so the inequality is strict with no equality, and it must point toward the region above the boundary line on the graph (larger values), giving $0.05x + 0.10y > 15$. Therefore, the answer is C."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T08:09:42.953Z","issues":[],"alt":"좌표평면(x -50~350, y -50~200), 가로축 Number of cans (x), 세로축 Number of bottles (y). 직선 0.05x + 0.10y = 15: y = −0.5x + 150."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R4-f9c182ee","fromVersionId":"2a870125-0c95-424a-96a8-bb300372443e","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R4-fc996258","problem_id":"fc996258-541a-48c7-897d-8a9ba9725535","from_version_id":"68f2e31b-2e1b-4fb2-b362-f41d8c4d8a2b","expect":{"explanation_en":"Using the two points P(0, 40) and Q(8, 80) on the graph, the slope is (80 - 40)/(8 - 0) = 40/8 = 5. Because y is in thousands of dollars, the value increases by 5 thousand dollars each year. Choice B ($8,000) mistakes the x-coordinate difference, 8, for the answer. Choice C ($40,000) mistakes the y-intercept (the initial value) for the rate of increase. Choice D ($10,000) divides the difference of the y-values, 40, by something other than 8. The answer is A."},"set":{"explanation_en":"Using the two points P(0, 40) and Q(8, 80) on the graph, the slope is (80 - 40)/(8 - 0) = 40/8 = 5. Because y is in thousands of dollars, the value increases by 5 thousand dollars each year. Choice B (\\$8,000) mistakes the x-coordinate difference, 8, for the answer. Choice C (\\$40,000) mistakes the y-intercept (the initial value) for the rate of increase. Choice D (\\$10,000) divides the difference of the y-values, 40, by something other than 8. The answer is A."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T08:09:42.954Z","issues":[],"alt":"좌표평면(x 0~10, y 0~100), 가로축 Years since acquisition, 세로축 Value (thousands of dollars). 직선 y: y = 5x + 40; 점 P(0, 40) (0, 40); 점 Q(8, 80) (8, 80)."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R4-fc996258","fromVersionId":"68f2e31b-2e1b-4fb2-b362-f41d8c4d8a2b","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R4-80571cb5","problem_id":"80571cb5-de3c-4ee5-925c-192a362a2b12","from_version_id":"2eae0891-d80d-4ef0-9532-c08dd5e359f4","expect":{"passage":"An economic historian examined annual per-capita income (in inflation-adjusted dollars) in a textile-manufacturing town during a period of industrial transition, comparing four selected years spanning a major economic shift. According to the graph, per-capita income was $410 in 1890, $380 in 1900, $520 in 1910, and $610 in 1920."},"set":{"passage":"An economic historian examined annual per-capita income (in inflation-adjusted dollars) in a textile-manufacturing town during a period of industrial transition, comparing four selected years spanning a major economic shift. According to the graph, per-capita income was \\$410 in 1890, \\$380 in 1900, \\$520 in 1910, and \\$610 in 1920."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T08:09:42.955Z","issues":[],"alt":"선그래프, 가로축 Year, 세로축 Per-capita income (dollars). Per-capita income: 1890 410, 1900 380, 1910 520, 1920 610."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R4-80571cb5","fromVersionId":"2eae0891-d80d-4ef0-9532-c08dd5e359f4","fromVersionNo":1}}],"sets":[{"set_group_id":"f981bddb-2e31-4bf0-bdc6-da8f68582ed0","set_id":"cb5e2094-7fcd-48fd-854e-b4db0a634165","name":"AP Microeconomics — Practice Exam 1","version_no":1,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["cad95f09-2fa9-4d87-8368-9e9cbf9b5116","e8cace9c-1f38-421d-9d02-a73fdd1da989","5a119626-c674-4721-a0a2-6053451abaaf","e0a53d0a-2f93-47e3-8f99-a3b91ebe4757","45b01834-a627-4058-8d6a-d4fae7cc7482","2f382df5-bec9-4c82-9ed7-abefa34000ef","9e36b933-3127-41ae-8f57-0d8a89ebae52","b83a1199-3d4f-4995-9f10-756ea2aae6f8","e679dc7a-7441-405c-9015-7da412e209fe","635cf96f-4df9-4170-8bf0-9b79695251ce","6ede1d00-b75b-425f-8a05-e9c0697b90b4","ce9ec65e-9f24-436a-a8ac-c2af6df55454","6971039a-1321-4bfd-8e6f-2a5d244cf07c","707149e1-4e26-4aa0-bd77-04e7b7c8a2ac","1e6fccc1-85e2-4c08-9579-5113857b05b0"],"patch_ids":["R4-cad95f09","R4-e8cace9c","R4-5a119626","R4-e0a53d0a","R4-45b01834","R4-2f382df5","R4-9e36b933","R4-b83a1199","R4-e679dc7a","R4-635cf96f","R4-6ede1d00","R4-ce9ec65e","R4-6971039a","R4-707149e1","R4-1e6fccc1"]},{"set_group_id":"1f165064-c082-4d5e-bd17-0a0f3863e6f8","set_id":"06f1e19e-c6b8-495c-9078-91eae82e3af3","name":"AP Microeconomics — Practice Exam 2","version_no":1,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["81f3e92f-3d26-4a67-b71f-a5ce4adf9540","af029b04-0310-4704-8988-8c454baedde2","71873d83-ee72-4b54-8b04-addf4fc0b3da","a8288d2d-4b19-45f1-939d-0f39e1297210","ef6d5033-a79c-4745-abf9-02c325295856","09e5da9b-6acc-4fca-a67d-77adef94bb2b","41e1e9a4-0c6b-411b-b96b-eb3bc09c718f","487f1f6b-1a18-42b1-ad0f-224e12408e13","c04ef1d1-10a4-490f-bd5d-76c4fcb7edff","ed9c4a43-caec-4ea0-85dd-bcbc1cb27c7d","06ed4f21-82fc-4bd2-8136-051eb2289d09","4960068e-860c-4e35-acf3-cd62aac48296","577b6861-c886-4237-bfa2-941cd4558cfa","20ff87c0-fe18-444e-a762-d5408e1dbaa5","cfcba202-77fa-4118-8e2a-bb82f61d7ad2","3a1d9a4d-e9cc-456c-a142-782757f8fb99","d7449d89-3222-4c96-be3e-bccf63f19ebe","07516ae5-0353-4b3f-976c-ec50de46dd9e"],"patch_ids":["R4-81f3e92f","R4-af029b04","R4-71873d83","R4-a8288d2d","R4-ef6d5033","R4-09e5da9b","R4-41e1e9a4","R4-487f1f6b","R4-c04ef1d1","R4-ed9c4a43","R4-06ed4f21","R4-4960068e","R4-577b6861","R4-20ff87c0","R4-cfcba202","R4-3a1d9a4d","R4-d7449d89","R4-07516ae5"]},{"set_group_id":"693168bd-3fb0-4cdf-8dc4-21186e80cf39","set_id":"c6d0390e-f43c-4695-ba3a-91cb75653f61","name":"SAT Practice Test 1","version_no":4,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["2c02a8a7-220c-4e67-869b-97a63ee81f72","4cc47e7c-e766-47d8-aa9b-ae16aeb96d71"],"patch_ids":["R4-2c02a8a7","R4-4cc47e7c"]},{"set_group_id":"f4f02828-f657-467c-91a2-169955e44869","set_id":"42ad8b42-646d-43b7-bae2-41f65e2ac7ab","name":"SAT Practice Test 2","version_no":4,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["8c87572d-4516-40ce-bedd-d51e92d01ea4","80571cb5-de3c-4ee5-925c-192a362a2b12"],"patch_ids":["R4-8c87572d","R4-80571cb5"]}]}$audit_plan$::jsonb;
  v_actor uuid := (select id from profiles where role = 'admin' order by created_at limit 1);
  u jsonb; s jsonb; a jsonb;
  v_prob problems%rowtype; v_cur problem_versions%rowtype; v_have jsonb; v_want jsonb; v_new uuid;
  v_src mock_exam_sets%rowtype; v_new_set uuid; v_cols text; v_cols_i text; v_pids uuid[];
  v_n int; v_stale int; v_bad text; v_done_p int := 0; v_skip_p int := 0; v_done_s int := 0; v_skip_s int := 0;
begin
  if v_actor is null then raise exception 'No admin profile found.'; end if;

  -- Phase 1: problems -> new published problem version each (same flow as the admin problem editor)
  for u in select * from jsonb_array_elements(v_plan->'problems') loop
    select * into v_prob from problems where id = (u->>'problem_id')::uuid;
    if v_prob.id is null then raise exception '[%] problem % not found', u->>'patch_id', u->>'problem_id'; end if;
    select * into v_cur from problem_versions where id = v_prob.published_version_id;
    if v_cur.id is null then raise exception '[%] problem % has no published version', u->>'patch_id', v_prob.id; end if;
    -- final field values = current published version, overridden by the planned new values
    a := jsonb_build_object('passage', v_cur.passage, 'question', v_cur.question, 'options', v_cur.options, 'correct_index', v_cur.correct_index, 'answers', v_cur.answers,
                            'explanation', v_cur.explanation, 'explanation_en', v_cur.explanation_en, 'figure', v_cur.figure, 'statements', v_cur.statements, 'difficulty', v_cur.difficulty,
                            'figure_checked', coalesce(v_cur.figure_checked, false), 'evidence_target', v_cur.evidence_target, 'evidence_span', v_cur.evidence_span,
                            'answer_rationale', v_cur.answer_rationale, 'distractor_error_types', v_cur.distractor_error_types);
    v_have := (select coalesce(jsonb_object_agg(k, a->k), '{}'::jsonb) from jsonb_object_keys(u->'expect') k);
    v_want := (select coalesce(jsonb_object_agg(k, (a || (u->'set'))->k), '{}'::jsonb) from jsonb_object_keys(u->'expect') k);
    if v_have = v_want then v_skip_p := v_skip_p + 1; raise notice '[%] already applied, skipped', u->>'patch_id'; continue; end if;
    if v_have <> u->'expect' then raise exception '[%] DRIFT: the published version of problem % no longer matches the expected old content (re-run the dry-run)', u->>'patch_id', v_prob.id; end if;
    a := a || (u->'set');
    if exists (select 1 from problem_versions where problem_id = v_prob.id and status in ('draft', 'in_review')) then
      raise exception '[%] problem % has an open draft/in_review version', u->>'patch_id', v_prob.id;
    end if;
    v_new := public.save_problem_draft_version(
      p_problem_id => v_prob.id, p_passage => a->>'passage', p_options => nullif(a->'options', 'null'::jsonb), p_correct_index => (a->>'correct_index')::int,
      p_explanation => a->>'explanation', p_difficulty => a->>'difficulty', p_actor_id => v_actor, p_answers => nullif(a->'answers', 'null'::jsonb),
      p_figure => nullif(a->'figure', 'null'::jsonb), p_figure_checked => coalesce((a->>'figure_checked')::boolean, false), p_statements => nullif(a->'statements', 'null'::jsonb),
      p_question => a->>'question', p_repair_status => null, p_explanation_en => a->>'explanation_en', p_evidence_target => a->>'evidence_target',
      p_evidence_span => a->>'evidence_span', p_answer_rationale => a->>'answer_rationale', p_distractor_error_types => nullif(a->'distractor_error_types', 'null'::jsonb));
    perform public.set_problem_render_check(v_new, u->'render_check');
    perform public.set_problem_quality(v_new, coalesce(v_cur.quality, '{}'::jsonb) || jsonb_build_object('auditFix', u->'audit_fix'));
    perform public.confirm_and_publish_problem_version(v_new, v_actor);
    if u ? 'ap' then perform public.ap_attach_new_version(u->'ap'->>'candidate_key', (u->'ap'->>'item_index')::int, v_new, v_actor); end if;
    v_done_p := v_done_p + 1;
    raise notice '[%] problem % -> new version %', u->>'patch_id', v_prob.id, v_new;
  end loop;

  -- Phase 2: sets -> new set version per affected published set (items of the patched problems point to the new problem versions; snapshots of the rest are copied)
  for s in select * from jsonb_array_elements(v_plan->'sets') loop
    select array_agg(x::uuid) into v_pids from jsonb_array_elements_text(s->'problem_ids') x;
    select * into v_src from mock_exam_sets where set_group_id = (s->>'set_group_id')::uuid and status = 'published' and archived_at is null order by version_no desc limit 1 for update;
    if v_src.id is null then raise exception 'Set group % has no published version (%).', s->>'set_group_id', s->>'name'; end if;
    select count(*) into v_n from mock_exam_set_items i where i.exam_set_id = v_src.id and i.problem_id = any (v_pids);
    if v_n <> cardinality(v_pids) then raise exception 'Set % v% holds % of % target problems.', v_src.name, v_src.version_no, v_n, cardinality(v_pids); end if;
    select count(*) into v_stale from mock_exam_set_items i join problems p on p.id = i.problem_id
     where i.exam_set_id = v_src.id and i.problem_id = any (v_pids) and p.published_version_id is distinct from i.problem_version_id;
    if v_stale = 0 then v_skip_s := v_skip_s + 1; raise notice 'set % v% already up to date, skipped', v_src.name, v_src.version_no; continue; end if;
    perform pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtextextended('mock_exam_new_version:' || v_src.set_group_id::text, 0));
    if exists (select 1 from mock_exam_sets where set_group_id = v_src.set_group_id and status = 'draft') then
      raise exception 'A draft version already exists for set % - publish or archive it first.', v_src.name;
    end if;
    v_new_set := gen_random_uuid();
    select string_agg(quote_ident(column_name), ', ' order by ordinal_position) into v_cols
      from information_schema.columns where table_schema = 'public' and table_name = 'mock_exam_sets' and is_generated = 'NEVER'
       and column_name not in ('id', 'version_no', 'status', 'created_by', 'created_at', 'published_at', 'published_by', 'archived_at', 'access_tier');
    execute format('insert into mock_exam_sets (id, version_no, status, created_by, access_tier, %s) select $1, $2, ''draft'', $3, ''tutoring'', %s from mock_exam_sets where id = $4', v_cols, v_cols)
      using v_new_set, (select max(version_no) + 1 from mock_exam_sets where set_group_id = v_src.set_group_id), v_actor, v_src.id;
    select string_agg(quote_ident(column_name), ', ' order by ordinal_position),
           string_agg(case column_name
                        when 'problem_version_id' then 'case when i.problem_id = any ($3) then coalesce(p.published_version_id, i.problem_version_id) else i.problem_version_id end'
                        when 'content_snapshot' then 'case when i.problem_id = any ($3) then null else i.content_snapshot end'
                        else 'i.' || quote_ident(column_name) end, ', ' order by ordinal_position)
      into v_cols, v_cols_i
      from information_schema.columns where table_schema = 'public' and table_name = 'mock_exam_set_items' and is_generated = 'NEVER' and column_name not in ('id', 'exam_set_id', 'created_at');
    execute format('insert into mock_exam_set_items (exam_set_id, %s) select $1, %s from mock_exam_set_items i join problems p on p.id = i.problem_id where i.exam_set_id = $2', v_cols, v_cols_i)
      using v_new_set, v_src.id, v_pids;
    if v_src.format = 'mst' and not coalesce((public.mock_exam_validate_mst_set(v_new_set)->>'ready')::boolean, false) then
      raise exception 'New version of % is not ready (4-module blueprint check failed).', v_src.name;
    end if;
    select string_agg(x.problem_id::text || ':' || x.issue, ', ') into v_bad from (select * from public.mock_exam_set_item_issues(v_new_set) where issue is not null limit 5) x;
    if v_bad is not null then raise exception 'New version of % has item issues: %', v_src.name, v_bad; end if;
    perform public.mock_exam_publish_set(v_new_set, v_actor);
    v_done_s := v_done_s + 1;
    raise notice 'set % v% -> v% (%)', v_src.name, v_src.version_no, v_src.version_no + 1, v_new_set;
  end loop;
  raise notice 'DONE problems applied=% skipped=%; sets applied=% skipped=%', v_done_p, v_skip_p, v_done_s, v_skip_s;
end
$audit$;
